## Goal
Show how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) cluster, using a PCA of the gene-level
counts coloured by condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |

Sample condition labels come from the design table `00_data/rnaseq_bulk/samples.csv` (`sample_id`, `condition`).

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib). Inputs are read-only.

1. Read the `counts_gene` matrix (genes x samples; `gene_id` and `gene_name` columns, then
   A1-A3, B1-B3) and the `condition` column of `samples.csv`; check the sample sets match.
2. Drop genes with zero counts in all samples; keep the rest.
3. Transform: log2(count + 1). The matrix is already length-scaled by the pipeline, so no
   further length or library-size normalisation is applied.
4. Select the 500 most variable genes (variance across samples of the log2 values).
5. PCA with scikit-learn on samples x genes, centred, not scaled to unit variance; all
   components (at most 5 for six samples).
6. Write the sample coordinates and explained variance per component to a table.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by condition (untreated / treated), points
   labelled with sample ID, axes labelled with percent variance explained. Save as PNG.

